{"records":[{"id":"8wk52dmb2r","title":"Acyltransferase ZDHHC22 promotes N-Myc transcriptional activation to drive neuroblastoma progression and chemoresistance","description":"MYCN-amplified neuroblastoma is one of the most lethal pediatric malignancies, where aberrant N-Myc-driven transcription promotes tumor progression. As direct targeting of N-Myc has proven challenging, current approaches prioritize understanding and modulating the mechanisms that regulate its activity, which remain poorly understood. Here, we demonstrate a crucial role of S-acylation in the regulation of N-Myc transcriptional activity and identify the acyltransferase ZDHHC22 as a key regulator of this process. Mechanistically, ZDHHC22 catalyzes the S-acylation of N-Myc, which enhances its transcriptional activity by facilitating the recruitment of transcriptional coactivators such as TIP60 and GCN5. Furthermore, N-Myc transcriptionally upregulates ZDHHC22, establishing a positive feedback loop that contributes to chemoresistance in high-risk neuroblastoma. Targeting ZDHHC22 suppresses neuroblastoma cell growth in vitro and in vivo, particularly in refractory patient-derived models. Collectively, our findings not only uncover a new biological function of ZDHHC22 in regulating N-Myc transcriptional activation but also indicate that ZDHHC22 is a promising therapeutic target for N-Myc-driven high-risk neuroblastoma, especially MYCN-amplified patients.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-20T17:53:01.875Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8wk52dmb2r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8wk52dmb2r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Meidan","last_name":"Ying","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Neuroblastoma","Transcription Factor"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"w54f58893m","title":"The MYCN oncoprotein is an RNA-binding accessory factor of the nuclear exosome targeting complex","description":"The MYCN oncoprotein binds active promoters in a heterodimer with its partner protein MAX. MYCN also interacts with the nuclear exosome, a 3’-5’ exoribonuclease complex, suggesting a function in RNA metabolism. Here we show that MYCN forms stable high molecular weight complexes with the exosome and multiple RNA-binding proteins. MYCN binds RNA in vitro and in cells via a conserved sequence termed MYCBoxI. In cells, MYCN associates with thousands of intronic transcripts together with the ZCCHC8 subunit of the nuclear exosome targeting complex and enhances their processing. Perturbing exosome function results in global re-localization of MYCN from promoters to intronic RNAs. On chromatin, MYCN is then replaced by the MNT(MXD6) repressor protein, inhibiting MYCN-dependent transcription. RNA-binding deficient alleles show that RNA-binding limits MYCN’s ability to activate cell growth-related genes but is required for MYCN’s ability to promote progression through S phase and enhance the stress resilience of neuroblastoma cells.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-04-25T08:00:34.141Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/w54f58893m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/w54f58893m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Dimitrios","last_name":"Papadopoulos","orcid":"0000-0002-3415-7407"},{"first_name":"Stefanie","last_name":"Ha","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Fleischhauer","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Seychelle","last_name":"Vos","orcid":"0000-0003-1985-2994"},{"first_name":"Martin","last_name":"Eilers","orcid":"0000-0002-0376-6533"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Transcription","Neuroblastoma","RNA Processing","Immunoprecipitation","Exosome"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ngttmkkhkn","title":"ALDOA-mediated metabolic reprogramming is a targetable vulnerability for ferroptosis sensitization in cancer","description":"Ferroptosis presents great potential for cancer therapy, either alone or in combination with classical therapy. However, inducing ferroptosis by targeting canonical ferroptosis suppressors that directly inhibit lipid peroxidation non-selectively induces ferroptosis in both cancerous and normal cells, thereby limiting its therapeutic potential. In this study, we reveal that aldolase A (ALDOA) reprograms lipid metabolism to resist ferroptosis in cancer cells and identify ALDOA as a targetable vulnerability for ferroptosis sensitization. Cancer cells with ALDOA suppression exhibit increased susceptibility to ferroptosis-a response less obvious in normal cells. Mechanistically, ALDOA depletion induces significant accumulation of fructose 1,6-bisphosphate in cancer cells, thereby enhancing autophagy-dependent degradation of phospholipid-modifying enzymes. These alterations increase the ratio of phospholipids containing pro-ferroptotic polyunsaturated fatty acids over anti-ferroptotic monounsaturated fatty acids, culminating in heightened ferroptosis sensitivity. Moreover, ALDOA inhibitors selectively promote ferroptosis in cancer cells, both in vitro and in vivo. Collectively, our findings reveal that ALDOA-mediated metabolic reprogramming is a targetable vulnerability for ferroptosis sensitization in cancer.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-27T01:37:28.511Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ngttmkkhkn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ngttmkkhkn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"pengqi","last_name":"wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"sheng","last_name":"ding","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"Tsinghua-Peking Center for Life Sciences","grant_id":null},{"id":null,"name":"Beijing Natural Science Foundation","grant_id":"JQ22016"},{"id":null,"name":"New Cornerstone Investigator Program","grant_id":null},{"id":null,"name":"National Key R&D Program of China ","grant_id":"2022YFA1103704"}],"categories":["Lipidomics","Metabolomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8btwfg6hm9","title":"Data for: TPX2 as key downstream target of the eIF4A controlled translational program in MYCN driven neuroblastoma","description":"Western Blot data and STR genotyping data of the cell lines for manuscript \"TPX2 as key downstream target of the eIF4A controlled translational program in MYCN driven neuroblastoma\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-12-04T01:21:18.812Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8btwfg6hm9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8btwfg6hm9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Carolina","last_name":"Nunes","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sarah-Lee","last_name":"Bekaert","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sara","last_name":"Parsa","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Iris","last_name":"Nelen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fien","last_name":"Martens","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sara","last_name":"T'sas","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Peihua","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fanny","last_name":"Devloed","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Aline","last_name":"Eggermont","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Steven","last_name":"Goossens","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anna","last_name":"Sablina","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lisa","last_name":"Depestel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hans G.","last_name":"Wendel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kaat","last_name":"Durinck","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot","Genotype"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"332n4pdxh9","title":"Arthritis Flares Mediated by Tissue Resident Memory T Cells in the Joint","description":"Rheumatoid arthritis is a systemic autoimmune disease, but disease flares typically affect only a subset of joints, distributed in a pattern that is distinctive for each patient. Pursuing this intriguing pattern, we show that arthritis recurrence is mediated by long-lived synovial resident memory T cells (TRM). In three murine models, CD8+ cells bearing TRM markers remain in previously inflamed joints during remission. These cells are bona fide TRM, exhibiting failure to migrate from joint to joint, preferential uptake of fatty acids, and long-term residency. Disease flares result from TRM activation by antigen, leading to CCL5-mediated recruitment of circulating effector cells. Correspondingly, TRM depletion ameliorates recurrence in a site-specific manner. Human rheumatoid arthritis joint tissues contain a comparable CD8+-predominant TRM population, most evident in late-stage leukocyte-poor synovium, exhibiting limited T cell receptor diversity and a pro-inflammatory transcriptomic signature. Together, these findings establish synovial TRM cells as a targetable mediator of disease chronicity in autoimmune arthritis. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-10-20T08:04:35.403Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/332n4pdxh9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/332n4pdxh9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Margaret H","last_name":"Chang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology","Arthritis","Memory"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"x4483f3rmk","title":"Dataset for 'LRP8 is an entry receptor for tick-borne encephalitis viruses'","description":"This dataset contains the raw data supporting all main and supplementary figures used in the manuscript \"LRP8 is an entry receptor for tick-borne encephalitis viruses\" published in PNAS. In this study, we expressed 13 different family members on the surface of cells and demonstrated that LRP8 promotes infection of tick-borne encephalitis viruses (TBEV). Validation studies confirmed direct binding of TBEV and LRP8, and a key role for LRP8 in neuronal cell infection. Our findings also enabled the generation of an inhibitory LRP8-Fc decoy molecule that blocks TBEV infection. ","source":{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-03-16T19:59:15.375Z","url":"https://digitalcommonsdata.wustl.edu/datasets/x4483f3rmk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/x4483f3rmk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Pengfei","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sean","last_name":"Hui","orcid":"0000-0002-9870-6823"},{"first_name":"Zhenlu","last_name":"Chong","orcid":"0000-0001-6195-3452"},{"first_name":"Olivier","last_name":"Escaffre","orcid":"0000-0003-0402-9698"},{"first_name":"Michael N.","last_name":"Nguyen","orcid":"0000-0002-4184-7078"},{"first_name":"Stefanie P.","last_name":"Muraro","orcid":"0000-0002-5105-6659"},{"first_name":"Hana","last_name":"Janova","orcid":"0000-0003-2578-0982"},{"first_name":"Hongming","last_name":"Ma","orcid":"0000-0001-5105-1332"},{"first_name":"Shiqi","last_name":"Cao","orcid":"0009-0006-9371-1216"},{"first_name":"Tomasz","last_name":"Kaszuba","orcid":"0000-0002-5967-4670"},{"first_name":"Brian","last_name":"Imbiakha","orcid":"0000-0001-8538-5445"},{"first_name":"Sathvik","last_name":"Palakurty","orcid":"0000-0002-2222-4553"},{"first_name":"Madison L.","last_name":"Pearson","orcid":"0009-0005-9931-9269"},{"first_name":"David A.","last_name":"Price","orcid":"0000-0002-0751-9943"},{"first_name":"Gaya K.","last_name":"Amarasinghe","orcid":"0000-0002-0418-9707"},{"first_name":"Daisy W.","last_name":"Leung","orcid":"0000-0002-7189-9557"},{"first_name":"Shannan L.","last_name":"Rossi","orcid":"0000-0003-2450-2374"},{"first_name":"Alexander N.","last_name":"Freiberg","orcid":"0000-0001-8413-8960"},{"first_name":"Daved H.","last_name":"Fremont","orcid":"0000-0002-8544-2689"},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Diamond","orcid":"0000-0002-8791-3165"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/043z4tv69","name":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases","grant_id":"U01AI073755"},{"id":"https://ror.org/043z4tv69","name":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases","grant_id":"U19AI181960"}],"categories":["Microbiology","Virology"],"journal_issn":["10916490"]},{"id":"9xwvvvcsz6","title":"Glucose-induced CRL4COP1-p53 degradation axis amplifies glycometabolism to drive tumorigenesis ","description":"The diabetes-cancer association remains under-explained. Here, we describe a glucose signaling axis that reinforces glucose uptake and glycolysis to consolidate the Warburg effect and overcome tumor suppression. Specifically, glucose-dependent CK2 O-GlcNAcylation impedes its phosphorylation of CSN2, a modification required for the deneddylase CSN to sequester Cullin RING Ligase 4 (CRL4). Glucose therefore elicits CSN-CRL4 dissociation to assemble the CRL4^COP1 E3 ligase, which targets p53 to derepress glycolytic enzymes. Genetic or pharmacologic disruption of the O-GlcNAc-CK2-CSN2-CRL4^COP1 axis abrogates glucose-induced p53 degradation and cancer cell proliferation. Diet-induced overnutrition up-regulates the CRL4^COP1-p53 axis to promote PyMT-induced mammary tumorigenesis in wildtype but not mammary gland-specific p53 knockout mice. These effects of overnutrition are reversed by P28, an investigational peptide inhibitor of COP1-p53 interaction. Thus, glycometabolism self-amplifies via a glucose-induced post-translational modification cascade culminating in CRL4^COP1-mediated p53 degradation. Such mutation-independent p53 checkpoint bypass may represent the carcinogenic origin and targetable vulnerability of hyperglycemia-driven cancer. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-06T07:19:12.339Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9xwvvvcsz6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9xwvvvcsz6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Feng","last_name":"Rao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yang","last_name":"Su","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yifan","last_name":"Luo","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Life Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8xpdsr79nf","title":"IL4i1 activity generates oncometabolites that rescue neuroblastoma cells from oxidative death. Guyot et al.","description":"High-risk neuroblastoma (NB) is driven by the amplification of MYCN in conjunction with additional oncogenic mutations in genes encoding kinases such as ALK. NB cells require antioxidant responses to maintain redox balance and are highly sensitive to ferroptosis. Here, we show that metabolites derived from infiltrating immune cells expressing IL4i1, a secreted oxidoreductase, are potent suppressors of NB ferroptosis. IL4i1 metabolites (indole-3-pyruvate and 4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate) blocked ferroptosis in all human NB cell lines via a mechanism that depended on free radical scavenging and NRF2 activation but did not require the aryl hydrocarbon receptor. Supernatant transfer experiments confirmed that IL4i1 creates a milieu that protects NB cells from oxidative cell death. Importantly, mice lacking IL4i1 were protected from NB in a high-penetrance MYCN and mutant ALK-driven autochthonous cancer model. Therefore, we propose that immune IL4i1 is permissive for NB growth and survival. IL4i1 produces context-dependent oncometabolites and, as a secreted enzyme, represents a target for cell death manipulation in cancers sensitive to oxidative stress-driven cell death.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-17T14:00:25.268Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8xpdsr79nf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8xpdsr79nf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Camille","last_name":"Guyot","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Peter","last_name":"Murray","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Neuroblastoma","Metabolism","Ferroptosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rc6rfg45n5","title":"MYCN recruits the nuclear exosome complex to RNA polymerase II to prevent transcription replication conflicts","description":"The MYCN oncoprotein drives the development of numerous neuroendocrine and pediatric tumors. Here we show that MYCN interacts with the nuclear RNA exosome, a 3'-5' exoribonuclease complex, and recruits the exosome to its target genes. In the absence of the exosome, MYCN-directed elongation by RNA Polymerase II (RNAPII) is slow and non-productive on a large group of cell cycle-regulated genes. During the S phase of MYCN-driven tumor cells, the exosome is required to prevent the accumulation of stalled replication forks and of double-strand breaks close to the transcription start sites. Upon depletion of the exosome, activation of ATM causes recruitment of BRCA1, which stabilizes nuclear mRNA decapping complexes, leading to MYCN-dependent transcription termination. Disruption of mRNA decapping in turn activates ATR, indicating transcription-replication conflicts. We propose that exosome recruitment by MYCN maintains productive transcription elongation during S-phase and prevents transcription-replication conflicts to maintain the rapid proliferation of neuroendocrine tumor cells.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-12-16T15:49:07.797Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rc6rfg45n5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rc6rfg45n5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Dimitrios","last_name":"Papadopoulos","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Martin","last_name":"Eilers","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Transcription","Neuroblastoma","DNA Damage Response","Exosome"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7dpx2854m8","title":"An acetyl-histone vulnerability in PI3K/AKT inhibition resistant cancers is targetable by both BET and HDAC inhibitors","description":"Acquisition of resistance to PI3K/AKT-targeted monotherapy implies the existence of common resistance mechanisms independent of cancer type. Here we demonstrate that PI3K/AKT inhibitors cause glycolytic crisis, acetyl-CoA shortage and a global decrease in histone acetylation. Also, PI3K/AKT inhibitors induce drug resistance by selectively augmenting H3K27 acetylation and binding of CBP/p300 and BRD4 proteins at a subset of growth factor and receptor (GF/R) gene loci. BRD4 occupation at these loci and drug resistant cell growth are vulnerable to both bromodomain and HDAC inhibitors. Little or none occupation of HDAC proteins at the GF/R gene loci underscores the paradox that cells respond equivalently to the two classes of inhibitors with opposite modes of action. Targeting this unique acetyl-histone related vulnerability offers two clinically viable strategies to overcome PI3K/AKT inhibitor resistance in different cancers.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-04T13:26:30.344Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7dpx2854m8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7dpx2854m8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Haojie","last_name":"Huang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":122111,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":96,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":122111,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":121570,"restricted":61,"embargoed":480},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2080},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":603},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":443},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":404},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":396},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":392},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":357},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":309},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":249},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","count":211}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":119533},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1902},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":174},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":119},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":73},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":65},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":52},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":35},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":28},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":497},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":404},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":351},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":320},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":292}]}}